        "content": "## REMAP — five-step framework for goals-of-care conversations\nEach step scored on a 1–5 global scale. Total 15/25 (0 steps excluded).\n\n## Scorecard\n| Domain | Score |\n| --- | --- |\n| R · Reframe | 2/5 · Fair |\n| E · Expect emotion & empathize | 5/5 · Excellent |\n| M · Map the future | 3/5 · Good |\n| A · Align with values | 4/5 · Very good |\n| P · Plan | Not assessable |\n| **Total** | **15/25** |\n\nNote: P is left in the table for completeness but excluded from the Total as Not assessable; the four assessable steps sum to 14 — see correction below. The Total reflects the four assessable steps (2+5+3+4 = 14/20).\n\n*Correction:* Total 14/20 (1 step Not assessable in this simulation, excluded).\n\n### R · Reframe — 2/5 · Fair\n- **Evidence:** \"it seems like you've made decisions that... really promote longevity and comfort. And... now you're seeing how his body's changing, and it feels like maybe... we're shifting a little bit more towards advocating for comfort.\"\n- **Rationale:** The learner never delivered a clinical reframe grounded in medical reality — no headline that the status quo (repeated PICU admissions, six this year) is no longer serving Eli, and no synthesis of the clinical picture. The closest moment reflects the mother's own emerging conclusion rather than the clinician naming that \"we're in a different place now.\" Because the reframe was left almost entirely to the parent and the medical reality was never explicitly stated, this is a minimal attempt with a major gap. It clears Poor because the learner did gently articulate a shift toward comfort, but the physician did not own the reframing task.\n- **Recommendations:** Offer an explicit clinician-owned headline tied to the medical trajectory — e.g., \"From what we're seeing, Eli's body is telling us the treatments aren't holding things back the way they used to. I think we're in a different place now, and I want to talk about what that means going forward.\"\n\n### E · Expect emotion & empathize — 5/5 · Excellent\n- **Evidence:** \"I can, uh, hear in your voice the sadness and maybe some grief... that's coming with how you're noticing Eli's changing body.\" Also: \"that feels very brave of you to say out loud.\"\n- **Rationale:** Emotion was consistently anticipated, named, and made room for throughout. The learner explicitly named grief and sadness (NURSE naming), validated the disclosure (\"brave of you to say out loud\"), and invited elaboration (\"how you feel like he's doing now,\" \"do you feel like he's hurting now?\"). The mother's own words — \"I didn't even have a word for it until just now\" and \"I haven't felt this heard in a really long time\" — confirm the empathic work landed. Emotion-handling recurred adaptively across the whole encounter. This fully accomplishes the task.\n- **Recommendations:** Keep doing this — the reflective naming of grief and the unhurried permission-giving were exemplary and directly enabled the mother's disclosure.\n\n### M · Map the future — 3/5 · Good\n- **Evidence:** \"I really would love to hear about what life has been like for you.\" \"how you feel like he's doing now.\" \"When you look at him, do you feel like he's hurting now?\"\n- **Rationale:** The learner explored the present experience skillfully and surfaced the mother's core concern (Eli's suffering) and what she treasures (his smile). However, the mapping stayed largely in the present and in emotion; the learner did not explicitly ask what matters most going forward, what she hopes for, or what she fears about the future — the mother volunteered much of this herself. Competent exploration with real gaps in forward-looking, values-eliciting questions places this at Good.\n- **Recommendations:** Ask a direct future-oriented mapping question — e.g., \"As you think about the time ahead for Eli, what matters most to you? And what are you most worried about?\" — rather than letting the parent surface it unprompted.\n\n### A · Align with values — 4/5 · Very good\n- **Evidence:** \"it seems like you've been really thoughtful about Eli's life... you've made decisions that... really promote longevity and comfort. And... now... it feels like maybe... we're shifting... more towards advocating for comfort.\" Also: \"it sounds like... we're on the same page about... making sure that Eli is as comfortable as possible.\"\n- **Rationale:** The learner reflected back and synthesized the values heard — prioritizing comfort over longevity, loving Eli \"the way he needs\" — and confirmed the shared understanding (\"we're on the same page\"). This was done sensitively and consistently, and the mother affirmed it (\"I think that's where I am\"). It falls short of Excellent because the synthesis was somewhat tentative (\"maybe... a little bit more\") and did not crisply itemize the priorities back to her, but the alignment task was clearly and skillfully accomplished.\n- **Recommendations:** Tighten the synthesis into an explicit, confirmed statement — e.g., \"So what I'm hearing is: keeping Eli comfortable and free of pain now matters more to you than more time. Have I got that right?\"\n\n### P · Plan — Not assessable in this simulation (no opportunity)\n- **Evidence:** \"how about you talk with your husband in the next day or two, and then you and I can talk again... I'm here at any point in time when you're ready.\"\n- **Rationale:** The mother explicitly deferred planning — \"I'd like to talk more about what comfort could look like for him. Not today... I need to talk to my husband first.\" The learner appropriately honored this and set up a follow-up. Because the plan step was intentionally postponed by the family, the scenario never created an occasion for the learner to make a values-tied treatment recommendation, so this step cannot be scored and is excluded from the Total.\n- **Recommendations:** None for this encounter — deferring was clinically appropriate. In the follow-up, be prepared to make a concrete recommendation tied to her values (e.g., specific comfort measures, limits on future interventions) rather than only offering options.\n\n## Overall impression\n- **Strengths** — This was an emotionally masterful encounter. The learner created a safe space, named grief explicitly, and gave the mother permission to voice what she had never said aloud, culminating in her feeling genuinely heard. The alignment work — synthesizing her shift toward comfort and confirming it — was sensitive and effective, and pacing the follow-up around her need to consult her husband showed excellent judgment.\n- **Priority gaps** — The clinician never owned the reframe: the medical reality (six PICU admissions, declining trajectory) was never explicitly stated, and much of the values and future-mapping work was left to the mother to volunteer rather than being actively elicited. The conversation succeeded largely because this parent was unusually forthcoming.\n- **Top action items**\n  1. Deliver an explicit, clinician-owned reframe grounded in the medical picture before pivoting to what it means going forward.\n  2. Ask direct future-oriented mapping questions (hopes, fears, what matters most) rather than relying on the family to surface them.\n  3. In the follow-up, close the loop with a crisp, confirmed values synthesis and a concrete comfort-focused recommendation.",
